# Game packs

Every new config is made from one of these files, or from "Start from
scratch". Picking a pack gives the config its game (locked for life), its
picture, its Smart Actions and its aim settings. Decision D115.

## Add a pack

1. Copy an existing file here, for example `apex-legends.json`, to
   `<your-id>.json`. The id is lowercase letters, digits and dashes, and it
   never changes once a config has been made from it.
2. Set `id` (the same as the file name), `name` (what the player sees, and the
   game the config is locked to, 64 characters at most) and `version: 1`.
3. Add the id to `index.json`, where you want it in the list.
4. Run `node dashboard/tools/test-game-packs.js`. It names every mistake.
5. Rebuild the exe and run `node dashboard/tools/sync-mobile.js`.

## Make one from a config you tuned by hand

Global Settings > Developer > **Save this config as a game pack**. It copies
the config's buttons, Smart Actions and aim settings into a pack file and
hands you the text. A config that was made from a pack keeps that pack's id
and gets the next version number. Save it here, then steps 3 to 5.

## Adding a game's curve to its preset (D284)

The customer's Velocity calibration only switches on for a game whose preset
carries a MEASURED `aimModel` (`needsCalibration: false`). Today only
`cod-bo2.json` does. The preset ships at the game's MAX in-game sensitivity,
so a player at max needs no calibration at all: the curve's SHAPE is
measured at a low sensitivity (where the frames resolve every stick), then
the SPEEDS at max. On a config made from the game's preset:

1. **Curve at low sensitivity.** Set the game to a LOW sensitivity, open
   Translation > **DEV ONLY — USE FOR LOGIC** > **Curve calibration** and
   run it. The Core is told the preset's id, so its model comes back named
   for it.
2. **Copy the shape.** When every check passed, tap **Copy for the game
   preset**: the model exactly as a pack's `aimModel` holds it (the pack id,
   `needsCalibration: false`, a dated first `sources` line with the run's
   numbers, the Core's sources, the preset's `gameSettings` when the run has
   none). Paste it to Claude, or save it and run
   `node dashboard/tools/set-pack-aim-model.js <pack-id> <file>`. When the
   preset's `gameSettings` name a sensitivity ("Look sensitivity: Highest")
   the tool refuses it, because these speeds are the low-sensitivity run's;
   add `--shape-first` to write it now (it prints a WARNING) and do steps
   3 to 5 straight after. Then reload the app (rebuild the exe: its packs
   are baked in) so it knows the new shape, and make sure the config runs
   that curve (**Use this translator** after the curve run, or **Use the
   <game> translator**): the speeds copy checks the run scaled the preset's
   own points.
3. **Set max sensitivity.** Set the game to the sensitivity the preset ships
   at (and the in-game dead zone it should assume).
4. **Velocity calibration.** Run the Velocity calibration card (above the
   fold).
5. **Copy the speeds.** When it passed, the fold's card offers **Copy max-sens
   speeds for the game preset**: type the in-game sensitivity (and dead zone)
   it was measured at, prefilled from the preset's rows, and tap it. It
   carries the preset's shape with the run's dead zone and turn rates per
   mode, a dated source line, and `measuredAt`, wrapped as
   `{"zerostickPresetSpeeds": 1, "pack", "measuredAt", "aimModel"}`. Paste it
   to Claude, or run `node dashboard/tools/set-pack-aim-model.js <pack-id>
   <file> --speeds`. It takes speeds only when the pack already has a
   measured shape and the model's curves ARE that shape's points; it writes
   the zones and rates (the shape kept), sets the `gameSettings` sensitivity
   and dead-zone rows to what the run was measured at, and says what
   changed.

Both copies refuse a run made for another game and say so when the Core no
longer has the run (a config's saved translator cannot be turned back into
a preset: it keeps no sources, settings or `accel`). A velocity run is never
a shape: "Copy for the game preset" refuses one, and the speeds copy refuses
a preset with no measured curve yet.

The tool's checks: format, id, measured, the numbers a game can have (zone
under 35 %, full stick 30 deg/s or more, looking up no faster than 1.5x
turning, aiming no faster than hip), a date in its sources, and the app's
own validators. Each write adds 1 to `version`, adds a dated sentence to
`notes`, and keeps the model it replaced in
`docs/calibration/<pack-id>-replaced-<date>.json`. `--dry-run` shows the
change first; `-` reads the model from stdin.

Then run `node dashboard/tools/test-game-packs.js` and `test-aim-model.js`,
rebuild the exe, and run `node dashboard/tools/sync-mobile.js`. Nothing else
needs touching: `index.json` lists ids only (unchanged), `sync-mobile.js`
copies `game-packs/` whole, and `export-presets.js` is the Smart Action
presets, not game packs. New configs start with the curve on; an existing
config of that game is offered "Use the <game> translator" (or "Update to the
latest ...") on the Translation tab, and its Velocity calibration switches
on at once (it reads the preset).

## Translators imported from XIM's data (D394)

`dashboard/tools/import-xim-translators.js` wrote the `aimModel`,
`aimVariants` and `status` of 16 packs (5 of them new: Black Ops 6, PUBG,
The Finals, Marathon, Rust) from the owner's XIM MATRIX extraction, plaintext
only. The full record per game is in `dashboard/game-translators/`; `--check`
says whether the packs still match it and `--remove` takes it all back out.
docs/XIM-TRANSLATOR-IMPORT.md says what each number means and how sure it is.

## Add a trained spot (D404)

A trained spot is where the Anti-Recoil Lab's Automatic does everything by
itself (the Core checks the view, aims, fires a magazine, times the fire
rate, traces the holes). Train it in the app (developer builds, developer
mode on: Anti-Recoil Lab > Automatic > Train this spot), download the spot
file and its picture, then:

    node dashboard/tools/add-recoil-spot.js <pack-id> <spot.json> <picture.jpg>

It checks the spot with the app's own validator, copies the picture into
`spots/`, lists it in `spots/SOURCES.md` and adds the spot to the pack's
`recoilSpots` (a spot trained again replaces the old one). Real frames only.
docs/HOW-TO-TRAIN-A-SPOT.md is the step-by-step.

## Edit one

Change the file and add 1 to `version`. Configs already made from it keep
what they were made with; only new configs get the change.

`art`, `aliases` and `recoilSpots` are the exception: none goes into a
config (a config records only the pack's id and version), so changing only
those needs no new version.

## The fields

| field | what it is |
|---|---|
| `zerostickGamePack` | always `1` |
| `id`, `name`, `version` | see above |
| `aliases` | optional: other names a player may have typed for the game before game packs, e.g. `["Black Ops 2", "BO2"]`. A config made before game packs is matched to its pack by its game's name or one of these, ignoring case and punctuation, and gets the pack's picture. No two packs may share one. |
| `art` | the game's pictures, one per shape: `{"wide": "<id>.png", "tall": "<id>-tall.png"}`, each a `.png` in `art/` with a row in `art/SOURCES.md`; or `null` for our own banner. The old single `"<id>.png"` still works and means the wide one. See below. |
| `aimPreset` | optional: the Match Your Game row to preselect (`zs`, `cod`, `fn`, `apex`, `d2`). Never applied by itself. |
| `aimModel` | optional (D164): the game's aim model, format `zs-aim-model/1` (docs/AIM-MODEL.md): its stick dead zone, response curve, turn rates, turn ramp and dampening, the in-game settings they assume, and where each number came from. The Core's translator inverts it. `id` must be the pack's id. |
| `recoilSpots` | optional (D404): the Anti-Recoil Lab's trained spots, at most 8, each `{"id", "name", "where", "say", "picture", "ref", "viewBox", "box", "thrLo", "thrHi", "minScore", "tol", "fov": {"hip", "ads"}, "distanceM", "ads", "adsMs", "fireMaxMs", "texture", "trainedAt", "source", "wall": {"lo", "grey", "hip"}, "ammoBox"}` (`wall` and `ammoBox`, D408: the wall the Core found when the spot was trained, its line's brightness floor and grey, and where the ammo counter is, passed to the wall step). Made by Train this spot and written in by `tools/add-recoil-spot.js`; `picture` is a file in `spots/`; `ref` is the Core's 48 x 27 reference thumbnail (1728 characters of base64). Never by hand. |
| `aimVariants` | optional (D394): the game's translator once per in-game curve option, `[{"key", "label", "setting", "aimModel"}]`, 2 to 8; `aimModel` is one of them (the default). The Translation tab offers them as buttons ("Aim Response Curve Type in the game: Standard / Linear / Dynamic") |
| `status` | D394: `ready` (a measured aimModel and AI scripts), `calibrated` (a measured aimModel, AI scripts not yet) or `notStarted`. Must agree with the data (`ZSPACKS.statusOf`). The game picker's three sections and the configs' badge say it; the LOCK is still D300's one list (`GAMES_OPEN`, Black Ops II) |
| `aiScripts` | D394: `true` once the game's AI scripts are done |
| `notes` | free text for you. The app never shows it. |
| `smartActions` | Ready-made Smart Actions to add, by template id, e.g. `{"template": "slidecancel", "enabled": false}`. Optional `values` (the sliders, in order) and `buttons`. |
| `config` | config fields set for every way to play |
| `inputs` | one block each for `controller`, `mnk` and `stick_mouse` |

Each block in `inputs` is:

```json
{ "calibrated": false, "why": "a sentence saying why not", "config": {} }
```

or, once you have really measured it:

```json
{ "calibrated": true, "source": "who measured it, how, and when", "config": { ... } }
```

`calibrated: true` needs a `source` and a `config` that sets something. The
app tells the player whether the settings are tuned for their game, so never
mark a block calibrated because it feels right.

A `config` may set only: `bindings`, `accessibilityActions`,
`mouseTranslation`, `keyboardMovement`, `stick`, `deadZones`, `curves`,
`smoothing`, `axisInvert`, `constraints`, `aiAdjustment`, `vision`. Objects
are merged into the new config; lists replace. The name, the console, the way
to play, the game, the light and the photo are always the player's.

A new config starts as a copy of the Default config (pad configs at a native
1:1 stick), then `config`, then the block for the chosen way to play, then
`smartActions`. If a block lists its own `accessibilityActions`, that list is
the whole of it and `smartActions` is skipped for that way to play.

## The pictures (D116, D121)

The app draws a game's picture in two shapes, so a pack names one picture
for each:

| shape | the picture | drawn on |
|---|---|---|
| `wide` | Steam's 460 x 215 header (about 2.14:1), title included | the Overview banner, the Configs list, the new-config wizard |
| `tall` | a 2:3 portrait cover, 600 x 900 | HOME, and the small square in the More menu |

`ZSPACKS.artFor(config, shape)` hands out the right one (`'wide'`,
`'tall'` or `'square'`), and falls back to the other shape when a pack lacks
one. Every slot draws its picture whole, contained over a blurred copy of
itself, so nothing is ever stretched or cropped: a missing shape only means
a smaller picture with soft edges.

Official sources only (`art/SOURCES.md` says which, and why). To add a
game's pictures: add one row per picture to the SOURCES.md table (file,
shape, game, the size to save it at, the official URL, `not yet`), then run
`dashboard\tools\fetch-pack-art.ps1` once the owner has said yes. It scales a
bigger source down to the row's size, refuses to scale one up or change its
shape, and writes the date in. `node dashboard/tools/test-game-packs.js`
then checks every picture is a PNG of its row's size and shape.

The pictures live in the app only (D120). The exe and the phone app carry
this folder (`sync-mobile.js` copies `game-packs/art/`); nothing about a
picture, its file name or its URL ever goes into a config, so none of it
reaches the Core.
